  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1218 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nThis particular stretch of the ongoing heatwave continues through\nat least Wednesday as the mid to upper level ridge stays centered\nover New Mexico. The ridge does shift slightly west after midweek\nand elongates to the north, which allows the jet stream to bow \nmore south towards our area. While temperatures remain notably hot\nthrough Wednesday, a minor downward trend in forecast \ntemperatures is noted during the second half of the week as the \nupper-level flow allows the opportunity for more cirriform clouds \nto move over the area and abate some of the heat. \n\nWeak disturbances in the flow will mostly serve to bring a few \nswaths of increased cloud cover aloft. The moisture supply aloft \nisn't great, but there is some opportunity for one of these \nperturbations to bring isolated rains. This would be most favored \nThursday afternoon/evening with consensus emerging for a weak \nshortwave to quickly track from the Texas Panhandle to our area \nwhile mid-level temperatures become less restrictive to convective\ndevelopment. Still, the meager moisture and weak forcing keep \nchances generally low and near or below 30 percent for most areas.\nOver the weekend, the flow aloft becomes less favorable to \napproaching disturbances as the ridge becomes more zonally \ndiffuse. The core of the hot airmass associated with the ridge is \nexpected to become displaced a little farther to the northwest. \nThis suggests temperatures by this upcoming weekend won't reach \nnearly as high as the early week, but highs between the upper 90s \nto low 100s will still be likely while the overall pattern holds \ndry.",
